1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
The Electric Kettle

2
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:09,960
That's four.

3
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:13,280
Come on, Tom Thomas! Just one more!

4
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:18,679
Come on now, Tom Thomas! I know you can do it!

5
00:00:20,359 --> 00:00:21,480
Five…

6
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:24,359
That doesn't count! That doesn't count!

7
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:26,879
No? Your chin was below the bar!

8
00:00:26,879 --> 00:00:30,919
Phew! That's all! I can't do anymore!

9
00:00:31,039 --> 00:00:31,960
You weakling!

10
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:33,879
You're the weakling! I'm not!

11
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:37,439
I just haven't eaten in a while and that's why I lost my strength!

12
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:39,759
You're a slave to food, Tom!

13
00:00:40,079 --> 00:00:44,599
And you see, that's the difference between us Fixies and you humans!

14
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:49,879
Many people wrongly assume that the only way Fixies could live

15
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:52,719
is by stealing food off of humans' tables.

16
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:56,000
Or worse yet, by stealing it from their refrigerators.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,799
That's just a lie! It's not true at all!

18
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,480
Fixies don't eat any kind of human food.

19
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:05,079
So then where in the world do the Fixies get their energy, you're wondering?

20
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,519
It's very simple.

21
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:10,079
A Fixie's entire life is connected with devices.

22
00:01:10,239 --> 00:01:12,680
Fixies not only live inside of devices,

23
00:01:12,799 --> 00:01:15,719
but they take care of them and help them live longer.

24
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:17,599
And in return for their help,

25
00:01:17,719 --> 00:01:21,159
these devices share part of their energy with the Fixies.

26
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:22,280
So there you go!

27
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:26,159
The Fixies help devices, and devices help the Fixies.

28
00:01:26,239 --> 00:01:30,560
Yes, we Fixies and machines have a symbiotic relationship.

29
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:33,359
So we don't eat leftovers like cockroaches,

30
00:01:33,719 --> 00:01:35,159
because we're Fixies!

31
00:01:37,519 --> 00:01:41,359
One, two, three, whoa!

32
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:48,719
How is it possible that a big boy like you doesn't know how to make any food for himself?

33
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:53,280
I'm able to cook, but I'm not allowed to turn on the stove.

34
00:01:53,439 --> 00:01:54,920
What can you make without it?

35
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,640
Oh yes! We have instant oatmeal!

36
00:01:58,079 --> 00:01:58,799
Look!

37
00:02:00,039 --> 00:02:01,319
Do you like oatmeal?

38
00:02:01,879 --> 00:02:02,920
You're joking!

39
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,319
Only my folks say oats are healthy and make you stronger.

40
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:09,520
Great! Well then how do you cook it?

41
00:02:09,759 --> 00:02:10,639
It's not hard.

42
00:02:10,759 --> 00:02:14,599
All you've got to do is add hot water, and I'm allowed to turn on the kettle.

43
00:02:14,719 --> 00:02:16,800
Stop and check if there's water in there.

44
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,280
If there's none, you can burn out the kettle.

45
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,200
It's got enough.

46
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:23,160
Then you can turn it on!

47
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,439
Hey, tell me, how does the kettle turn off?

48
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:31,400
I mean, how does it know when the water is hot enough?

49
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:39,040
Inside of an electric kettle there's a heater hidden underneath its bottom.

50
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:43,480
When you turn on the kettle, the heater warms up the water until it boils.

51
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:46,320
And the boiling water gives off steam

52
00:02:46,319 --> 00:02:49,879
that heats up a special metal plate at the top of the kettle.

53
00:02:50,039 --> 00:02:54,199
The heat causes the metal plate to bend and that turns off the switch.

54
00:02:54,719 --> 00:02:59,319
So you could say that an electric kettle feels when the water is boiling.

55
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:06,919
OK, now I understand.

56
00:03:07,479 --> 00:03:09,280
Hey, why do you need three bowls?

57
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:11,560
You don't need to make any oatmeal for us.

58
00:03:11,759 --> 00:03:14,439
It's not for you guys. It's for my mom and dad.

59
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:16,400
Start out here.

60
00:03:17,079 --> 00:03:17,919
No!

61
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,159
Keep pouring into this one!

62
00:03:20,599 --> 00:03:22,199
And I say pour it here.

63
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:25,120
And I say first you should pour it into mine!

64
00:03:26,759 --> 00:03:28,960
Oh, Nolik! Where are you?

65
00:03:29,079 --> 00:03:31,079
I'll find him. Hang on, I'm going in.

66
00:03:34,079 --> 00:03:34,879
Simka!

67
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,479
She was right over here.

68
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:40,319
Nolik!

69
00:03:40,599 --> 00:03:41,680
Over here.

70
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:44,120
Simka!

71
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:44,920
Here!

72
00:03:45,599 --> 00:03:46,520
Nolik!

73
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:47,400
There!

74
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:57,719
Hey, where is your other boot? It got lost.

75
00:03:57,840 --> 00:03:59,200
Up there in the oatmeal.

76
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:04,439
That must be your parents.

77
00:04:04,719 --> 00:04:05,800
Let's get out of here.

78
00:04:05,960 --> 00:04:07,719
Hey, and what about your shoe?

79
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:09,759
Don't worry, I got another one.

80
00:04:09,879 --> 00:04:11,400
Hi, Tom Thomas. We're back.

81
00:04:11,759 --> 00:04:12,920
You must be hungry.

82
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:14,199
We'll make you something to eat.

83
00:04:14,199 --> 00:04:16,319
But I already prepared us some food.

84
00:04:16,479 --> 00:04:18,079
And the water is already hot.

85
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:19,319
Wash your hands!

86
00:04:19,399 --> 00:04:21,959
Tom Thomas, don't touch that kettle if it's hot.

87
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:23,560
I don't want you to burn yourself.

88
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:31,199
So, today we're eating oatmeal for dinner.

89
00:04:31,439 --> 00:04:32,039
Delicious!

90
00:04:32,599 --> 00:04:34,240
Uh, maybe you have something else?

91
00:04:34,319 --> 00:04:35,560
Why something else?

92
00:04:35,639 --> 00:04:38,560
You're the ones that say that oatmeal is great for you

93
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:40,039
and that it makes us stronger.

94
00:04:40,039 --> 00:04:42,719
Well, yeah, that's what we say.

95
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:46,280
I'm glad that our son pays such careful attention.

96
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:50,800
Isn't it delicious?

97
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:52,440
Really?

98
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:54,760
Huh? What's that?

99
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,960
Oh, look! You found the boot, Dad!

100
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:57,800
What?

101
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:03,720
Uh, it's nothing. Just eat your food and don't get distracted.

102
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:06,440
I'd like to see that oatmeal all gone, OK?

103
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:10,280
And whoever doesn't finish won't get any candy.

